# Commit 2: Process Direct adapter support

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Audience**: same as [`commit1_jdbc-support-and-naming-fixes.readme`](commit1_jdbc-support-and-naming-fixes.readme)
— written for both future Claude sessions and human contributors, explaining
the evidence and reasoning behind each decision, not just the diff.

---

## 1. What this commit adds: Process Direct support

The compiler previously had no Process Direct adapter. This adds it,
following the exact same evidence-based methodology as the JDBC work in
commit 1 — a real SAP-exported Process Direct iFlow ZIP
(`process_direct_reference.zip`, `.iflw`: *"Send Outbound Sales Order Status
from S4HANA to OCE"*) was inspected first, cross-validated against a second,
independent real export (the JDBC reference ZIP from commit 1, which also
happens to contain a Process Direct **Receiver** usage) before writing any
code.

### Process Direct's dual nature (the key finding)

Unlike JDBC (Receiver-only — a database can't push into CPI), the evidence
shows Process Direct genuinely has **two independent usages** in real SAP
flows:

1. **Flow-level adapter, both directions.** `Participant_1` ("S4HANA",
   `ifl:type="EndpointSender"`) → `MessageFlow_6` → `StartEvent_2`, i.e. a
   normal Sender, wired exactly like HTTP/SOAP/SFTP/IDoc via
   `flow.setSender()`/`setReceiver()`. Process Direct **can** be a Receiver
   too (SAP-internal, in-memory, no network hop — used to call *another*
   integration flow's start event directly).
2. **Mid-flow call component, Receiver-direction only.** `ServiceTask_56`
   ("RR_ErrorDetails", `activityType=ExternalCall` — structurally identical
   to `JdbcCall`'s serviceTask) → `MessageFlow_59` → `Participant_58`
   ("GlobalExceptionHandling", `ifl:type="EndpointRecevier"`). This is a
   request-reply call to *another* iFlow from the middle of processing,
   exactly the same shape as `JdbcCall`.

Both usages were confirmed, not assumed — the second (mid-flow) shape is
also present in the JDBC reference ZIP from commit 1 (`MessageFlow_61`,
identical `cmdVariantUri`), which is what made it safe to treat as a stable
pattern rather than a one-off.

**A version asymmetry was also confirmed across both reference exports**:
the Sender `cmdVariantUri` ends `.../version::1.1.2`, the Receiver ends
`.../version::1.1.1` — a real, evidenced distinction, not a typo, so both
are preserved exactly in `ProcessDirectAdapter.getCmdVariantUri()`.

**New files**:
- `src/model/ProcessDirectAdapter.ts` — the flow-level adapter
  (`.sender()`/`.receiver()` factories), plus the shared property set
  (`ComponentType=ProcessDirect`, `TransportProtocol`/`MessageProtocol=Not
  Applicable`, `ComponentNS=sap`, `Vendor=SAP`, `componentVersion=1.1`,
  `ComponentSWCVName=external`, `ComponentSWCVId=1.1.2`) taken verbatim from
  the reference exports. Only `address` (required) and `system` (optional,
  defaults to the display name) are accepted — confirmed against
  `parameters.propdef` in the reference ZIP, which lists `address` as the
  **only** externalizable Process Direct attribute.
- `src/model/ProcessDirectCall.ts` — the mid-flow component, mirroring
  `JdbcCall.ts` exactly: a `Component` subclass carrying a
  `ProcessDirectAdapter` (always built via `.receiver()`, matching the
  evidenced mid-flow direction).

**Modified to wire it in**: `ComponentRegistry.ts` (`ProcessDirectCall`
entry, identical `ExternalCall` flowstep metadata to `JdbcCall`),
`ComponentFactory.ts` (`ProcessDirectCall` component type, `ProcessDirect`
adapter type, both directions), `IFlow.ts` (`ProcessDirectAdapter` added to
the `sender`/`receiver` field types — the one place its behavior differs
from `JdbcAdapter`, which is receiver-only), `capabilities.ts`,
`validate.ts`, `index.ts`.

### Avoiding duplication with JDBC (`BpmnProcessMapper.ts`)

The previous JDBC-only inline block in `BpmnProcessMapper.ts` (build the
companion `EndpointRecevier` participant + messageFlow for every
`JdbcCall`) was generalized into one shared private method,
`mapMidFlowAdapterCall()`, parameterized by a default channel name and an ID
prefix, and keyed off a small structural interface (`MidFlowAdapterCall`)
rather than a common base class:

```typescript
components.forEach(component => {
    if (component instanceof JdbcCall) {
        this.mapMidFlowAdapterCall(component, "JDBC", "Jdbc", collaboration, usedChannelNames);
    } else if (component instanceof ProcessDirectCall) {
        this.mapMidFlowAdapterCall(component, "ProcessDirect", "ProcessDirect", collaboration, usedChannelNames);
    }
});
```

This was a deliberate choice to satisfy "do not duplicate existing
writer/mapper logic" — any future mid-flow adapter-call type (a third one
would need evidence first) can reuse the same method. Diagram/shape
positioning (`createDiagram()`) needed **no changes** — it already
generically handles any extra participant/messageFlow pair via its existing
mid-flow-adapter stacking logic.

---

## 2. ID collision safety (the recurring risk area)

Given the prior `CP-001` duplicate-ID history with `ProcessCall` (fixed in
commit 1), Process Direct's ID generation was built to be collision-safe
from the start, using a **distinct prefix** from every other ID space:

- `IdGenerator.next("Participant_ProcessDirect")` /
  `IdGenerator.next("MessageFlow_ProcessDirect")` — distinct from JDBC's
  `_Jdbc` prefix, from the fixed literals (`Participant_1`/`Participant_2`),
  and from `ProcessCall`'s own `Component.id` space (a completely separate
  ID axis).
- Component IDs (AI-supplied or generated) go through the same
  `ComponentFactory.resolveComponentId()` path as every other component
  type — no special-casing needed.
- Channel names go through the same `ensureUniqueTechnicalName()` dedup as
  every other adapter, against the same shared `usedChannelNames` set — so
  a flow with **both** a flow-level Process Direct adapter and a mid-flow
  `ProcessDirectCall` correctly gets `"ProcessDirect"` and
  `"ProcessDirect_2"`, not a collision.

Verified directly (not just asserted) in `test-processdirect-regression.ts`:
- 2 `ProcessDirectCall` instances in one flow → 0 `CP-001` errors, 2 unique
  `serviceTask` ids, 2 unique `messageFlow` ids, 2 unique participant
  targets, deduped channel names.
- 3 `ProcessCall` + 2 `ProcessDirectCall` instances mixed in one flow → 0
  duplicate IDs across all 49 BPMN element ids in the generated `.iflw`.
- `JdbcCall` + `ProcessDirectCall` in the same flow → exactly one `JDBC` and
  one `ProcessDirect` messageFlow, non-colliding ids.

---

## 3. Validation hardening (`AD-009`)

Added to `src/api/validate.ts`, following the same pattern as `AD-006`/
`AD-008` from commit 1:

- **`AD-009`** — Process Direct `address` must be a relative path beginning
  with `/`, checked in **both** directions (unlike `AD-006`, which is
  Sender-only for HTTP). Runs against the actual `ProcessDirectAdapter`
  instance produced by `fromJson()`, including the mid-flow `ProcessDirectCall`
  case (which, like `JdbcCall`, isn't visible via `getSender()`/`getReceiver()`
  and needs its own check in the component loop).
- `KNOWN_ADAPTER_VERSIONS` extended: `'ProcessDirect': { Sender: '1.1',
  Receiver: '1.1' }`.

Addresses without a leading `/` are **normalized** (a `/` is prepended), not
rejected — this matches the existing convention for HTTP(S) sender
addresses and is intentional, confirmed by a passing regression test rather
than left as an assumption.

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only)

**New**:
- `src/model/ProcessDirectAdapter.ts`
- `src/model/ProcessDirectCall.ts`
- `test-processdirect-regression.ts`

**Modified**:
- `src/api/capabilities.ts`, `src/api/validate.ts`
- `src/factory/ComponentFactory.ts`
- `src/index.ts`
- `src/mapper/BpmnProcessMapper.ts` (JDBC-only mid-flow block generalized
  into a shared `mapMidFlowAdapterCall()` method, used by both `JdbcCall`
  and `ProcessDirectCall` — verified not to change JDBC's own generated
  output via the full commit-1 regression suite)
- `src/model/IFlow.ts` (`ProcessDirectAdapter` added to `sender`/`receiver`
  field types)
- `src/registry/ComponentRegistry.ts` (`ProcessDirectCall` entry)

**Deliberately not touched**: writers (`ServiceTaskWriter.ts` from commit 1
already handles the `ProcessDirectCall` serviceTask — no new writer type was
needed, since both `JdbcCall` and `ProcessDirectCall` compile to the same
`ExternalCall` flowstep shape), the BPMN IR, packaging logic, existing
resource handling (Process Direct has no resources of its own — confirmed
by `parameters.propdef` showing only `address`), the Fiori UI, the CAP
service layer, and the root-level `src/` mirror (same reasoning as commit
1 — see `COMPILER_PACKAGE_MIGRATION.md`).

---

## 5. Testing

`test-processdirect-regression.ts` (plain `ts-node` script, same convention
as commit 1's suites), 13 scenario groups, all passing:

1. `getCapabilities()` exposes `ProcessDirectCall` (component) and
   `ProcessDirect` (adapter, both directions).
2. Process Direct as a flow-level Sender **and** Receiver — validated,
   compiled, and the raw `.iflw` inspected directly for correct
   `cmdVariantUri` version asymmetry (`1.1.2` Sender / `1.1.1` Receiver).
3. Single mid-flow `ProcessDirectCall` + `ContentModifier` — raw `.iflw`
   inspected for `serviceTask`/`ExternalCall`, messageFlow property set,
   `EndpointRecevier` participant, valid NCName channel name.
4. Two `ProcessDirectCall` instances — no duplicate ids anywhere, deduped
   channel names.
5. `Router` routing to two different `ProcessDirectCall` targets.
6. `ProcessDirectCall` + `ProcessCall` coexistence.
7. `ProcessDirectCall` + `JdbcCall` coexistence.
8. Mixed 3×`ProcessCall` + 2×`ProcessDirectCall` stress test (0 duplicate
   ids across 49 total BPMN element ids).
9. Missing `address` rejected.
10. Unsupported property (e.g. `timeout`) rejected.
11. Address without leading `/` is normalized, not rejected (documented
    behavior, matches HTTP convention).

Every structural claim above was checked against the **raw generated
`.iflw` XML**, not just `validate()`'s return value, and cross-checked
property-by-property against the reference ZIP evidence quoted in §1 —
same standard as commit 1.

**Full pre-existing regression suite re-run after this change — zero
regressions**:
- `test-jdbc-regression.ts`, `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`,
  `test-naming-version-regression.ts`, `test-customer-flow-regression.ts`
  (all from commit 1 — specifically re-verified because
  `BpmnProcessMapper.ts`'s JDBC code path was refactored, not just added
  to, in this commit).
- `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts` (9/9), `test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts`
  (3/3).

Run with (from `packages/compiler/`):
```
npm run build   # or: npx tsc --noEmit
npx ts-node test-processdirect-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-naming-version-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-customer-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-rt-003-tests.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts
```

---

## 6. Known limitations

- The flow-level Sender/Receiver participant is always named generically
  "Sender"/"Receiver" rather than using the configured `system` name for
  the participant label. This is pre-existing behavior shared by **every**
  adapter type (not Process-Direct-specific) and was intentionally left
  unchanged — the reference export's `Participant_1` is named "S4HANA", but
  changing participant naming for all adapters is outside this task's scope
  and has no evidence pinning down whether it should apply universally.
- `componentVersion=1.1` / `ComponentSWCVId=1.1.2` are pinned to the
  reference export evidence in this repo, same caveat as JDBC's version in
  commit 1 — a newer tenant profile would need a fresh export to confirm,
  not a guess.
- No new resource type was introduced for Process Direct (none needed per
  evidence), but if a future real export shows one, it should be verified
  the same way before being added.
